const readingMaterial={people:{title:"Australia and its people",paras:["Australia’s story begins long before European settlement. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples are Australia’s First Peoples, with diverse continuing cultures, languages, traditions and connections to Country.","European settlement began with the First Fleet in 1788. Separate British colonies later developed, and on 1 January 1901 six colonies federated to form the Commonwealth of Australia. Settlement and Federation are therefore different historical events.","Australia has six states and two mainland territories. Canberra is the national capital. Important national references include Australia Day on 26 January, Anzac Day on 25 April, the golden wattle, and the national colours green and gold."]},rights:{title:"Democratic beliefs, rights and liberties",paras:["Australia is a parliamentary democracy. Citizens elect representatives, while people can participate through voting, discussion, lawful campaigning and peaceful protest.","The rule of law means everyone is subject to Australian law. No individual, organisation, official, cultural practice or religious rule is above the law.","Freedoms include speech and expression, religion and association, exercised within Australian law. Equality of opportunity and the idea of a fair go reinforce dignity, respect and fair opportunity."]},government:{title:"Government and the law",paras:["The Australian Constitution establishes the basic framework for the Commonwealth system of government. Keep the three powers separate from the three levels of government.","Legislative power makes laws, executive power puts laws into action, and judicial power interprets and applies laws. A proposed new law introduced into Parliament is called a Bill.","The Australian Government deals with national matters such as defence, immigration and currency. States and territories manage services such as public hospitals and police. Local councils deal with services such as parks and rubbish collection."]},values:{title:"Australian Values",paras:["Values questions ask you to apply principles to situations. A useful filter is whether behaviour is lawful, peaceful, respectful and consistent with equality.","Respect for freedom and dignity includes accepting lawful differences. Equality means men and women have equal rights and people should have equality of opportunity. A fair go reflects fair opportunity and mutual respect.","Political disagreement should be pursued peacefully through voting, discussion, lawful campaigning and protest. Australian law remains the governing standard if a family, cultural or religious practice conflicts with it."]}};
